cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Hardware Recovery of the Analytic Pauli-Transfer-Matrix Structure of
  One-Axis-Twisting Boundary Channels
message: >-
  If you use this software, data, or the experimental protocols
  in this repository, please cite it as below.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "O'Néill"
    given-names: "Caoimhín"
    # orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"   # add before release
repository-code: "https://github.com/IllI/twisted-spin-ptm"
abstract: >-
  Theory, exact simulation, pre-registration, and three runs on IBM Quantum
  hardware measuring the predicted Pauli-transfer-matrix element
  T_xx = A cos^(N-2)(chi t / 2) of the one-axis-twisting boundary-pair
  channel, including its exact null at chi t = pi. Three identities are
  proved from the closed-form boundary density matrix (T_zz = 0, T_yy = 0,
  and the T_xx form), with the corollary that phase-only local corrections
  cannot exceed the classical fidelity bound. Hardware results: a 12.5-sigma
  signed ordering across three points, a nine-point sweep recovering the
  functional form at R^2 = 0.986 with the internal null at 0.4 sigma, and a
  disjoint-layout replication at R^2 = 0.976. Raw shot counts, calibration
  matrices, and job IDs are archived for every run; the entire analysis
  reproduces from counts upward. Includes a narrative account of the research
  path (docs/DISCOVERY_NARRATIVE.md) recording the falsified conjectures,
  retracted claims, and dry-run-caught circuit errors alongside the results
  that survived.
keywords:
  - quantum information
  - one-axis twisting
  - spin squeezing
  - Pauli transfer matrix
  - quantum channels
  - entanglement
  - IBM Quantum
  - superconducting qubits
  - tensor networks
  - pre-registration
  - reproducibility
  - state-space models
  - D-LinOSS
